1
00:00:00,302 --> 00:00:06,720
Okay, so this past weekend, DeepSeek released something that a lot of people out there are calling the answer to Claude code.

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:19,760
It's an agent that sits on a GitHub repo, it watches the issues and pull requests, and when someone on your team asks it to do something, it then goes off into its own session, does the work, and comes back with a PR.

3
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:24,960
Now, while that's cool in itself, that's not really the part that I want to talk about here.

4
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:28,320
The part that I find really interesting is how they built it.

5
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:33,988
The whole thing is just a set of plugins dropped into their agent harness.

6
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:38,960
And to understand why that matters, we need to go back and actually look at the harness itself.

7
00:00:39,406 --> 00:00:43,120
So when the Deep Seek harness came out back in August, I didn't actually cover it.

8
00:00:43,406 --> 00:00:48,640
And honestly, the reason for that was I just got so busy playing with it and trying out new ideas with it all the time.

9
00:00:48,814 --> 00:00:53,600
I kept putting off making the video because I felt like there were so many things that I wanted to show.

10
00:00:53,902 --> 00:01:02,916
For me, the whole plug-in architecture of that harness is really cool and it just allows you to do a whole bunch of things like this new Super Agent enables.

11
00:01:03,278 --> 00:01:06,560
Now, obviously, in the meantime, a lot of people ended up covering it.

12
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:10,000
It's turned out to be one of the most starred projects on GitHub this year.

13
00:01:10,318 --> 00:01:15,680
So in this video, I'm going to do a sort of catch-up on that and look at some of the new stuff that they've actually built on top of this.

14
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:19,440
All right, so the new thing that DeepSeq has launched is called Super Agent.

15
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:29,040
And along with the repo that they launched, they've basically written an article on X called Exploring Super Agent Design Towards a More Autonomous Agent System.

16
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:34,480
in that article in the repo, they're very upfront about that this is a research prototype.

17
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,612
It's not an official Deep Seek product.

18
00:01:37,006 --> 00:01:46,980
And they even mentioned that it grew out of their own day-to-day development work, and that a lot of the ideas and the engineering here were actually written by coding agents themselves.

19
00:01:47,566 --> 00:01:55,680
So the basic idea is this instead of you opening a session and talking to an agent, the agent listens to where your team already works.

20
00:01:55,886 --> 00:02:04,324
And the example that they've launched now that happens to be GitHub and also happens to be an app called Feishu, which is Bydance's sort of Slack equivalent.

21
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:22,320
Now, when someone who's allowed to asks it for something, it opens up a work item, it spins up its own session in its own directory, does all of the work, replies back to the original conversation, and then usually in that reply, it's going to have a pull request in there.

22
00:02:22,574 --> 00:02:34,116
And in many ways, you can kind of think of this as building on the whole ideas of software factories, of automated software generation and stuff that we've seen a lot of people being talking about over the past couple of months.

23
00:02:34,606 --> 00:02:39,520
Now, DeepSync themselves say that they've been running an internal version of this continuously.

24
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:49,120
It's been reproducing and fixing bug issues, responding to code review comments, and it's actually been making improvements to the DeepSync harness itself.

25
00:02:49,486 --> 00:02:56,240
So before I go into showing you the how of how it actually all works, I want to show you why the harness layer matters so much.

26
00:02:56,654 --> 00:03:06,160
Recently on the Venture Beat Podcast Beyond the Pilot, which I co-host, we've interviewed a number of interesting people, including the CTO of Instacart and the CTO of Box.

27
00:03:06,414 --> 00:03:10,160
Now, both of those episodes are coming up over the next couple of weeks or so.

28
00:03:10,382 --> 00:03:17,200
But one of the questions that I put to both of them was basically, what's more important now, the model or the harness?

29
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:20,320
Both of them came back on the side of the harness.

30
00:03:20,494 --> 00:03:24,676
And I'll leave it for you to watch the full interviews because it was fascinating conversations.

31
00:03:25,038 --> 00:03:31,520
But clearly now a lot of the innovation has moved away from just the model itself to the actual harnesses.

32
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:35,332
And maybe it will swing back in the future as we get better models.

33
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:38,756
But currently, you need to be focused on where the harnesses are going.

34
00:03:39,118 --> 00:03:44,880
All right, so one of the things that my team and I are trying to do right now is increase the coverage and the number of videos that we're doing.

35
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:49,200
It would really help if you just support the channel by clicking that subscribe button.

36
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:53,440
We know that most of you are watching a lot of the videos and still not subscribed.

37
00:03:53,742 --> 00:03:56,560
Anyway, back to why the harness is so important.

38
00:03:57,038 --> 00:04:00,400
So this is the Frontier Harness chart and result table.

39
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:02,080
So these aren't my numbers.

40
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:05,060
This comes from a benchmark called Frontier Harness eval.

41
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:14,640
And what they did was take one model, the Kimmy K3, and run it through nine different coding agent harnesses, 12 configurations in total.

42
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:19,760
Codex, Clawed Code, Pi, OpenCode, Deep Seek Harness, etc.

43
00:04:20,142 --> 00:04:24,080
They then gave it the same 30 tasks, same model, same runtime.

44
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:26,820
The only thing that changed here was the harness.

45
00:04:27,214 --> 00:04:38,640
Now you could make the argument that it's not a totally fair comparison, that Claude Code is optimized for its models, that Codex is optimized for the models, but it's really interesting to see the results that they got here.

46
00:04:38,894 --> 00:04:43,044
Codex came out on top with 66.7 pass rate.

47
00:04:43,342 --> 00:04:52,880
But if you look at that next row, Deep Seek Harness in its creator configuration and Claude Code both scored 63.3%.

48
00:04:53,520 --> 00:05:05,520
That's the same pass rate, same model, but the Deep Seek Harness run costs only about $3.28 per successful task, and Claude Code cost about $18.34.

49
00:05:05,838 --> 00:05:13,600
Now across the whole table, the gap between the cheapest and most expensive configurations was actually about 17.5x.

50
00:05:14,222 --> 00:05:17,680
Now I would be careful reading too much into the exact configurations here.

51
00:05:17,902 --> 00:05:21,892
It's 30 tasks, so one task is worth about three and a third points.

52
00:05:22,286 --> 00:05:26,720
The difference between Codex and Deep Seek is literally just one task here.

53
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:31,920
But I do think the big picture holds true here, that the harness isn't just a wrapper.

54
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:39,600
It changes the cost, the caching, the speed, and the success rate, even when the model is held constant.

55
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:47,440
So if you're evaluating a model, I'd try and do it in two or three harnesses before deciding whether the model is any good.

56
00:05:47,822 --> 00:05:52,400
Otherwise, you might be just blaming a model for what actually is the harness's responsibility.

57
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:55,760
All right, so what is DeepSeq Harness and why does it do well here?

58
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:04,560
So DeepSeek Harness or DSH came out on August 13th alongside the initial release of DeepSeek version 4 Pro.

59
00:06:04,654 --> 00:06:07,280
It's MIT licensed and there's web UI.

60
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:11,280
So you can start with just one NPX command and a desktop app.

61
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:13,840
It's not locked to the DeepSeek models.

62
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:16,164
I think that's really important to stress here.

63
00:06:16,494 --> 00:06:21,060
It works with other providers and even with OpenAI compatible endpoints.

64
00:06:21,486 --> 00:06:26,480
The key tagline here though that sort of sums this up is that everything is a plugin.

65
00:06:27,022 --> 00:06:29,360
And they mean that very literally.

66
00:06:29,614 --> 00:06:39,120
In most agent frameworks, you've got a core, the agent loop, the model calls, the tool handling, and then plugins sort of hang off on the side.

67
00:06:39,374 --> 00:06:42,560
In DSH, the router itself is a plugin.

68
00:06:42,990 --> 00:06:46,400
The tool registry, a plugin, the session log, a plugin.

69
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:49,680
Even the agent loop itself is a plugin.

70
00:06:50,254 --> 00:06:52,868
Underneath all of this is a framework called Cordis.

71
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:57,760
And there's a paper behind that from three researchers at the Beijing University.

72
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:03,840
And this framework, DeepSeek, called a programming paradigm for spatio-temporal composability.

73
00:07:04,142 --> 00:07:07,812
Now that sounds like a lot, but the two ideas here are actually pretty simple.

74
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:11,040
The first is what they call temporal composability.

75
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:15,108
When you remove a component, everything it did gets completely undone.

76
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:21,028
If a plugin registered a tool or a hook into event, unloading it reverses all of that.

77
00:07:21,326 --> 00:07:26,000
The simplest way to think about this is that you can unplug something really cleanly.

78
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:29,200
The second element here is spatial composability.

79
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:36,640
Components declare what they depend on, and the runtime activates or deactivates them as those dependencies come and go.

80
00:07:36,974 --> 00:07:41,040
So you can basically plug in things safely and unplug them safely as well.

81
00:07:41,358 --> 00:07:43,268
Now, why does an agent harness need this?

82
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:45,360
Because systems are changing constantly.

83
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:50,640
You're swapping models, you're adding tools, you're turning things on and off per permission.

84
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:56,560
And if every one of those changes sort of leaves junk behind, things get messy really quickly.

85
00:07:56,878 --> 00:08:01,600
We saw this even when people sort of reverse engineered how Clawed Code was working.

86
00:08:01,934 --> 00:08:05,680
Everyone that's making agent harnesses at the moment is moving at such a fast pace.

87
00:08:05,934 --> 00:08:09,040
It's just a simple fact that often code gets left behind.

88
00:08:09,390 --> 00:08:11,280
Anyway, so how does this look in practice?

89
00:08:11,470 --> 00:08:17,828
Well, running DSH is actually a tree of plugins, which gets composed when it boots up from layers.

90
00:08:18,254 --> 00:08:30,880
First come bundles, which are package sets of plugins plus their configs, then your profiles patch, a YAML file called cortis.patch, then you've got a home level patch, and then anything you pass in on the command line.

91
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:38,240
So each layer here can target any plugin by its ID and replace its config or insert new ones.

92
00:08:38,766 --> 00:08:46,948
They've even got a command in there for dump config that just prints out the whole tree that your machine is booting from, and every row in that can be overridden.

93
00:08:47,182 --> 00:08:51,008
Now in the UI, they've got four modes and they work in the same way.

94
00:08:51,168 --> 00:08:54,368
You've got standard, PTC, minimal, and creator.

95
00:08:54,638 --> 00:08:57,888
And those themselves are just different sets of plugins.

96
00:08:58,062 --> 00:09:01,508
The creator mode is sort of the logical endpoint for this.

97
00:09:01,742 --> 00:09:09,248
You can describe a plugin in chat and the agent will write it, install it, and then check that it's actually live.

98
00:09:09,486 --> 00:09:18,084
So you can kind of think of this as while everyone's been focused on just continual learning for models, this is bringing continual learning into the harness.

99
00:09:18,542 --> 00:09:23,728
Another thing that's worth knowing here is that the core repo here isn't taking outside pull requests now.

100
00:09:23,918 --> 00:09:32,208
They're pointing people to discussions and building plugins instead, which actually makes total sense for this design, right?

101
00:09:32,368 --> 00:09:36,804
If there's no core to patch, then plugins are how you contribute.

102
00:09:37,008 --> 00:09:39,728
And that's what brings us back to the whole super agent.

103
00:09:40,014 --> 00:09:45,728
So if you open the super agent repo, one of the first files you'll see is the cortis.patchyaml file.

104
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:48,128
And that file is basically the whole integration.

105
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:54,724
It inserts a core super agent plugin, a GitHub Watcher plugin, and a Facial Watcher plugin by default.

106
00:09:54,990 --> 00:09:57,728
Now, obviously, if you're not using those, you could just turn those off.

107
00:09:58,030 --> 00:10:02,368
But the way Super Agent works is that it defines a separate preset for the workers.

108
00:10:02,528 --> 00:10:07,728
You can think of it as DSH's standard preset with a bunch of things stripped out.

109
00:10:07,888 --> 00:10:14,288
No asking the person questions, no plan mode, no web fetch, no spinning off subagents, etc.

110
00:10:14,638 --> 00:10:20,208
And the shell is set so that any command running longer than five seconds gets pushed to a background job.

111
00:10:20,494 --> 00:10:26,208
That way a long build doesn't stop the worker from noticing that someone just left a new comment as it's going through.

112
00:10:26,542 --> 00:10:40,128
Obviously, that can be one of the frustrating things with Claude code and Codex is once it starts going, you're sitting there waiting to basically update it and you perhaps want to inject a prompt in while it's still going as you realize it's going in the wrong direction.

113
00:10:40,398 --> 00:10:44,368
So what you're looking at in this agent is a fairly different system entirely.

114
00:10:44,448 --> 00:10:47,396
It's built from configurations and plugins.

115
00:10:47,758 --> 00:10:53,808
There's no forking of the harness, and really that's the whole payoff of the DSH design that we've just talked about.

116
00:10:54,158 --> 00:11:01,728
Now, DSH itself did actually have an experimental agent teams feature with a roster, a taskboard, a mailbox.

117
00:11:01,888 --> 00:11:03,204
So why build this?

118
00:11:03,726 --> 00:11:08,928
Well, you can think about agent teams is about coordinating agents inside one job.

119
00:11:09,198 --> 00:11:19,968
The super agent is about handling lots of independent jobs that arrive from the outside world over perhaps a long period of time and also from lots of different people posting here.

120
00:11:20,206 --> 00:11:24,288
It's actually addressing a different problem than what the Teams feature did.

121
00:11:24,448 --> 00:11:29,568
And one cool practical detail here is that the router and the workers can each be given a different model.

122
00:11:29,774 --> 00:11:31,588
So I'll come back to that in a second.

123
00:11:31,982 --> 00:11:36,516
Now, if we look at the architecture here, this basically has two layers to it.

124
00:11:36,688 --> 00:11:39,888
On top, there's one long-running session that they call the router.

125
00:11:40,288 --> 00:11:48,004
It handles all the human interaction, keeps the big picture context, hands out work to perhaps different plugins or different agents.

126
00:11:48,398 --> 00:11:52,804
Underneath are those worker agents, each running in their own isolated session.

127
00:11:53,088 --> 00:11:55,328
Workers never have to talk to people directly.

128
00:11:55,488 --> 00:11:59,168
They report back to the router, the router does all the communication.

129
00:11:59,408 --> 00:12:02,448
Everything going in from the outside is a trigger.

130
00:12:02,638 --> 00:12:06,528
A new issue, a review comment, a chat message, etc.

131
00:12:06,894 --> 00:12:10,916
Each trigger first goes through a gate, which is plain code and not even a model.

132
00:12:11,150 --> 00:12:20,928
And that gives you a deterministic feature to basically check is this person even on the allowed list to be able to comment or to be able to file a pull request, etc.

133
00:12:21,198 --> 00:12:24,996
It also helps it to answer things like did they actually address the agent?

134
00:12:25,230 --> 00:12:31,328
Now only when those checks are passed does the model actually get to look at it and decide, you know, whether it's real work.

135
00:12:31,566 --> 00:12:34,820
If it is, it becomes a ticket with what they call a mandate.

136
00:12:35,008 --> 00:12:40,048
That's a record of who asked for it, under which rules, how far the work is allowed to go, etc.

137
00:12:40,334 --> 00:12:43,888
That ticket and the mandate then get passed to a worker.

138
00:12:44,174 --> 00:12:46,448
Now, here's a really cool detail that they've got in there.

139
00:12:46,688 --> 00:12:50,768
Once a worker owns a ticket, it subscribes to that conversation.

140
00:12:51,328 --> 00:12:56,448
So when someone comments on the PR that it opens, that comment goes straight to the worker.

141
00:12:56,718 --> 00:12:58,288
It skips the router entirely.

142
00:12:58,574 --> 00:13:05,648
And you can think about that skipping of that orchestration layer and going straight through just saves you a huge amount on tokens, etc.

143
00:13:05,968 --> 00:13:11,808
In fact, when they turned off this in their experiments, router decisions actually went up by 30%.

144
00:13:12,174 --> 00:13:14,608
And obviously the amount of tokens went up with it.

145
00:13:14,894 --> 00:13:19,888
For me, the other part that I find most interesting here though is about the router as the bottleneck.

146
00:13:20,142 --> 00:13:28,208
So we would assume that one central session that's doing the orchestration, handing everything out, would just get overwhelmed as things get busy.

147
00:13:28,368 --> 00:13:31,328
It actually turns out here that it kind of does the opposite.

148
00:13:31,488 --> 00:13:38,848
When triggers arrive while the router is in mid-inference, they pile up and they get handled together in the next call.

149
00:13:39,118 --> 00:13:48,208
And because the way that they've structured that router's job to be sort of long input and short on output, it actually allows it to batch these things really cheaply.

150
00:13:48,462 --> 00:13:54,608
Now in Deep Seek stress tests, they push the trigger rate from about one a minute to about 40 a minute.

151
00:13:54,830 --> 00:13:57,956
Average batch size went from one to 2.8.

152
00:13:58,382 --> 00:14:03,488
And sure, each call did get a bit slower, from about 2.5 seconds to 3.9.

153
00:14:03,808 --> 00:14:12,292
But the time per decision, because it's being done now in batches, actually drops from 6.4 seconds down to 1.6 seconds.

154
00:14:12,608 --> 00:14:15,168
So it got more efficient under load.

155
00:14:15,566 --> 00:14:21,728
They also say that the router job is often simpler than you'd expect, and a lighter model usually handles it fine.

156
00:14:21,998 --> 00:14:31,364
That kind of goes against the grain for a lot of thinking of where people have been thinking that your orchestrator needs to have the best model and that your sub-ages can get away with simpler models.

157
00:14:31,726 --> 00:14:34,756
It turns out that that router doesn't even need a bash tool.

158
00:14:35,086 --> 00:14:38,448
So overall, that router can use a fast, cheap model.

159
00:14:38,766 --> 00:14:41,408
It can actually use a stronger model for some of your workers.

160
00:14:41,646 --> 00:14:45,248
And this is something that I definitely want to test more with open models going forward.

161
00:14:45,550 --> 00:14:49,968
Anyway, on top of this, they have this philosophy, which I think is a really useful part.

162
00:14:50,128 --> 00:14:57,328
The principle is take as little human attention as possible and give as much value as possible.

163
00:14:57,710 --> 00:15:01,488
And I kind of feel as we're building agents, it's really hard to argue against that.

164
00:15:01,710 --> 00:15:07,876
You want an agent that only acts when someone allowed asks for it or when a rule the team wrote actually allows it.

165
00:15:08,078 --> 00:15:24,528
Interestingly, even in there, when work nobody asks for starts, it actually starts in a mode where it just records what it would have done It then confirms itself whether that would have actually worked, and then only speaks up when there's a result or a decision that somebody needs to make.

166
00:15:31,022 --> 00:15:32,528
Okay, so what's missing here?

167
00:15:32,688 --> 00:15:34,756
Let me go through a few caveats here.

168
00:15:35,022 --> 00:15:37,888
First, this is a prototype, and they say that really clearly.

169
00:15:38,094 --> 00:15:40,928
The published code isn't identical to their internal version.

170
00:15:41,198 --> 00:15:46,128
Some designs in the article are actually future directions that aren't in the repo yet.

171
00:15:46,350 --> 00:15:52,740
And from what I can see, things like the watchdog session perhaps isn't fully there yet, and I'm sure there are others that are missing as well.

172
00:15:53,166 --> 00:15:55,008
Second, this is all kind of narrow.

173
00:15:55,150 --> 00:15:56,768
It's one GitHub repo.

174
00:15:56,974 --> 00:16:00,036
They haven't set this up to work with Slack or with email.

175
00:16:00,334 --> 00:16:02,128
But actually, I'm not really worried about that.

176
00:16:02,318 --> 00:16:08,048
Because everything is basically a plugin, they've also included a cookbook for adding your own source code in there.

177
00:16:08,398 --> 00:16:12,928
So my guess is very quickly we will see forks of this with lots of other integrations in there.

178
00:16:13,262 --> 00:16:25,088
Third up, the thing that we should be a little bit concerned about is that the super agent experiments that they ran on and reported their benchmarks on are actually run on synthetic workloads in a simplified environment.

179
00:16:25,518 --> 00:16:29,028
And they say themselves that this is basically just a preliminary reference.

180
00:16:29,390 --> 00:16:32,144
And on things like the 30 tasks, this is just a small sample.

181
00:16:32,304 --> 00:16:34,624
So just treat those rankings as a rough guide.

182
00:16:34,958 --> 00:16:38,224
Finally, the big thing to be a little bit concerned about here is security.

183
00:16:38,478 --> 00:16:47,184
They do list the problems of prompt injection, credentials leaking, permissions across devices, etc., as things that they haven't fully solved yet.

184
00:16:47,534 --> 00:16:57,344
So naturally, you probably want to be a bit careful about how you use this, but if you are using it with local models in a totally locked down environment, you probably don't have a lot to really worry about here.

185
00:16:57,760 --> 00:17:06,224
All right, so overall, while I think Super Agent is a really interesting product here, what is even more interesting here is just basically the proof of design.

186
00:17:06,574 --> 00:17:16,784
Deep Seekers built something which is quite a different agent system on top of their own harness with just a few patch files and plugins without touching the core of the harness at all.

187
00:17:17,166 --> 00:17:23,024
And for me, the bigger point here is just showing you how much harnesses are evolving at such a quick pace at the moment.

188
00:17:23,264 --> 00:17:29,264
Anyway, as always, I've put the links to the repos, the paper, the benchmark, all of that's in the description.

189
00:17:29,518 --> 00:17:31,184
Let me know in the comments what you think.

190
00:17:31,344 --> 00:17:45,984
Do you see the future of the harnesses of being these composable plugins where different people create different ideas in different ways, and we're not just reliant on the same coding agent interfaces that we've seen up until now.

